  "research_id": "transaction-native-accounting",
  "title": "Accounting built from shared business events",
  "question": "Can signed business events compile payment, asset, accounting and assurance views continuously, and what evidence would establish that transition as real?",
  "bluf": "Accounting is moving from piecing together scattered records after the fact to building the books from shared business events. Licenses, accounting labels, audit reports and corrections are also being used to decide who may act, invest or rely on the result.",
  "evidence_cutoff": "2026-10-05",
  "evidence_cutoff_note": "October 5 adds the bounded state CPA licensure baseline and five-wave Accounting Permission Constitution. The licensure count is derived from the named entries in NASBA's October 1 tracker: 46 enacted jurisdictions, 38 effective by October 5 and eight future-dated through January 1, 2027. The permission study maps representative primary-source gates rather than claiming a census of every accounting-dependent permission in law and contracting. It preserves proposal, authority, qualification, exercise, reliance, correction and scale as separate states. The CPA designation and transfer-agent modernization remain proposals; revised PCAOB AS 2901 and QC 1000 remain future-effective through December 14; no complete named operating stack or correction propagation case is established.",
  "use_note": "Use for hypothesis history and internal retrieval only. The current answer routes through Private Monetary Stack, and this receipt does not establish government open-ledger adoption, smart-contract automation, or operating transaction-native accounting.",
